om/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/commit/eea5836bcdac1d5f3b720bc713833014385e3741","node_id":"C_kwDOUmZXItoAKGVlYTU4MzZiY2RhYzFkNWYzYjcyMGJjNzEzODMzMDE0Mzg1ZTM3NDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/commit/29a5a69fbeeec62c895b87ffcc6cad658cf2d769","sha":"29a5a69fbeeec62c895b87ffcc6cad658cf2d769","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/commits/29a5a69fbeeec62c895b87ffcc6cad658cf2d769"}],"sha":"eea5836bcdac1d5f3b720bc713833014385e3741","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/commits/eea5836bcdac1d5f3b720bc713833014385e3741"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/29927851?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/martmull","id":29927851,"login":"martmull","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI5OTI3ODUx","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/martmull","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/commits/29a5a69fbeeec62c895b87ffcc6cad658cf2d769/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-16T14:47:57Z","email":"martmull@hotmail.fr","name":"martmull"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-16T14:47:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(last-contact): run database event triggers in batch mode (#26072)\n\n## Problem\n\n`last-contact` listens to `messageParticipant.updated`. A mailbox sync\nemits one event per synced message, and every event enqueued its own\nlogic function job, which overflowed the workers on large syncs.\n\n## What changed\n\nEvery database event trigger in the app now sets `batchMode: true`, so a\nbatch of events arrives as a single job instead of one job per event:\n\n| Logic function | Trigger |\n| --- | --- |\n| `on-email-interaction` | `messageParticipant.updated` |\n| `on-calendar-interaction` | `calendarEventParticipant.updated` |\n| `on-person-created` / `on-person-updated` | `person.created` /\n`person.updated` |\n| `on-company-created` | `company.created` |\n| `on-opportunity-created` / `on-opportunity-updated` |\n`opportunity.created` / `opportunity.updated` |\n\nBatching only helps if the handler does not simply loop the old\nper-event work, so each handler now folds its batch first:\n\n- Events are deduplicated down to the distinct records they touch:\n`(personId, messageId)` pairs, `(personId, calendarEventId)` pairs,\ncompany ids, opportunity ids. 200 messages for the same person become\none person write, not 200.\n- Message, meeting and opportunity lookups use `in` queries over the\nwhole batch instead of one round trip per record: one paginated\n`messageParticipants` query resolves every message of the batch, one\n`calendarEventParticipants` query every meeting, one `opportunities` +\none `people` query the whole opportunity recompute. The company\nrecompute keeps one indexed lookup per company — finding a company''s\nmost recently contacted person has no batched \"top 1 per company\" form,\nso an `in` query there would page through every contacted person of\nevery company in the batch.\n- `updatePersonForInteractions` folds several interactions for one\nperson into a single guarded write, keeping the existing forward-only\nsemantics (newest contact, newest inbound, newest outbound, newest\nemail, newest meeting).\n- Companies shared by several people in a batch collapse into one\n`updateCompanies` call carrying the most recent contact among them.\n- `timeoutSeconds` goes to 300 on the batched handlers, since one run\nnow covers a whole batch.\n\nTwo things came along with it:\n\n- A calendar interaction now resolves the participant''s own calendar\nevent instead of re-querying the person''s most recent past meeting.\nUnder the same recency guards the result is identical, and it drops a\nper-person query. A participant on a future event is still left to the\n`on-calendar-event-started` cron, which also moved onto the shared\nbatched path.\n- Every read and write in these paths goes through `executeWithRetry`.\nThis matters more under batching than it did before: at one event per\njob a transient API error cost a single event, whereas a batch job that\nthrows leaves every record after the failure unprocessed.\n\n## A note on how meetings are read\n\nThe batched meeting collector first resolved its events through the\n`calendarEvents` root query, and every meeting assertion in the\nintegration suite went red. That query only returns events attached to a\ncalendar channel the caller can see, so an event created straight\nthrough the API is invisible there:\n\n```\nfilter id-in on 3 seeded events        -> 3 returned\nsame filter + 1 freshly created event  -> 3 returned, new event NOT visible\n```\n\nThe same event is reachable nested under\n`calendarEventParticipant.calendarEvent`, which is how the app read\nmeetings before this PR. The collector now reads `startsAt` /\n`isCanceled` from there and filters past and canceled events in code,\nwhich also folds the two queries into one.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n`batchMode` needs server support, so `engines.twenty` moves to\n`>=2.40.0` and `twenty-sdk` / `twenty-client-sdk` to `2.40.0`. App\nversion bumped to 1.3.0 with a changelog entry.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Integration suite 20/20 against `twentycrm/twenty-app-dev:latest`, the\nimage CI uses. Also green on both CI variants (`dockerhub-latest` and\n`local`).\n- `yarn test:unit` — 45 passing, including new cases for batch folding\n(one person write for several messages, one company write for people\nsharing a company, one query per batch) and for skipping future and\ncanceled meetings.\n- `yarn typecheck`, `yarn lint` — clean.\n\n## Known follow-up\n\nThe chunk → `first`/`after` → `endCursor` pagination loop is now written\n11 times in this app (5 of them pre-existing). A `collectAllPages(ids,\nfetchPage)` helper would cut ~15 lines from each site with each caller''s\nquery left verbatim. Left out of this PR to keep it to one concern;\nworth a focused follow-up that can absorb the pre-existing copies too.","tree":{"sha":"0f783ffb0b9eeb25afb83ecd1a0dc6a5547ce88a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/git/trees/0f783ffb0b9eeb25afb83ecd1a0dc6a5547ce88a"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Desync-ai/twenty-desync/git/commits/29a5a69fbeeec62c895b87ffcc6cad658cf2d769","verification":{"payload":"tree 0f783ffb0b9eeb25afb83ecd1a0dc6a5547ce88a\nparent 1ac815b13e449ca5386ca768115b6ef5bf85db38\nauthor martmull <martmull@hotmail.fr> 1789570077 +0000\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1789570077 +0000\n\nfeat(last-contact): run database event triggers in batch mode (#26072)\n\n## Problem\n\n`last-contact` listens to `messageParticipant.updated`. A mailbox sync\nemits one event per synced message, and every event enqueued its own\nlogic function job, which overflowed the workers on large syncs.\n\n## What changed\n\nEvery database event trigger in the app now sets `batchMode: true`, so a\nbatch of events arrives as a single job instead of one job per event:\n\n| Logic function | Trigger |\n| --- | --- |\n| `on-email-interaction` | `messageParticipant.updated` |\n| `on-calendar-interaction` | `calendarEventParticipant.updated` |\n| `on-person-created` / `on-person-updated` | `person.created` /\n`person.updated` |\n| `on-company-created` | `company.created` |\n| `on-opportunity-created` / `on-opportunity-updated` |\n`opportunity.created` / `opportunity.updated` |\n\nBatching only helps if the handler does not simply loop the old\nper-event work, so each handler now folds its batch first:\n\n- Events are deduplicated down to the distinct records they touch:\n`(personId, messageId)` pairs, `(personId, calendarEventId)` pairs,\ncompany ids, opportunity ids. 200 messages for the same person become\none person write, not 200.\n- Message, meeting and opportunity lookups use `in` queries over the\nwhole batch instead of one round trip per record: one paginated\n`messageParticipants` query resolves every message of the batch, one\n`calendarEventParticipants` query every meeting, one `opportunities` +\none `people` query the whole opportunity recompute. The company\nrecompute keeps one indexed lookup per company — finding a company''s\nmost recently contacted person has no batched \"top 1 per company\" form,\nso an `in` query there would page through every contacted person of\nevery company in the batch.\n- `updatePersonForInteractions` folds several interactions for one\nperson into a single guarded write, keeping the existing forward-only\nsemantics (newest contact, newest inbound, newest outbound, newest\nemail, newest meeting).\n- Companies shared by several people in a batch collapse into one\n`updateCompanies` call carrying the most recent contact among them.\n- `timeoutSeconds` goes to 300 on the batched handlers, since one run\nnow covers a whole batch.\n\nTwo things came along with it:\n\n- A calendar interaction now resolves the participant''s own calendar\nevent instead of re-querying the person''s most recent past meeting.\nUnder the same recency guards the result is identical, and it drops a\nper-person query. A participant on a future event is still left to the\n`on-calendar-event-started` cron, which also moved onto the shared\nbatched path.\n- Every read and write in these paths goes through `executeWithRetry`.\nThis matters more under batching than it did before: at one event per\njob a transient API error cost a single event, whereas a batch job that\nthrows leaves every record after the failure unprocessed.\n\n## A note on how meetings are read\n\nThe batched meeting collector first resolved its events through the\n`calendarEvents` root query, and every meeting assertion in the\nintegration suite went red. That query only returns events attached to a\ncalendar channel the caller can see, so an event created straight\nthrough the API is invisible there:\n\n```\nfilter id-in on 3 seeded events        -> 3 returned\nsame filter + 1 freshly created event  -> 3 returned, new event NOT visible\n```\n\nThe same event is reachable nested under\n`calendarEventParticipant.calendarEvent`, which is how the app read\nmeetings before this PR. The collector now reads `startsAt` /\n`isCanceled` from there and filters past and canceled events in code,\nwhich also folds the two queries into one.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n`batchMode` needs server support, so `engines.twenty` moves to\n`>=2.40.0` and `twenty-sdk` / `twenty-client-sdk` to `2.40.0`. App\nversion bumped to 1.3.0 with a changelog entry.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Integration suite 20/20 against `twentycrm/twenty-app-dev:latest`, the\nimage CI uses. Also green on both CI variants (`dockerhub-latest` and\n`local`).\n- `yarn test:unit` — 45 passing, including new cases for batch folding\n(one person write for several messages, one company write for people\nsharing a company, one query per batch) and for skipping future and\ncanceled meetings.\n- `yarn typecheck`, `yarn lint` — clean.\n\n## Known follow-up\n\nThe chunk → `first`/`after` → `endCursor` pagination loop is now written\n11 times in this app (5 of them pre-existing). A `collectAllPages(ids,\nfetchPage)` helper would cut ~15 lines from each site with each caller''s\nquery left verbatim. Left out of this PR to keep it to one concern;\nworth a focused follow-up that can absorb the pre-existing copies too.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqqwtCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAApCAQABFXuZDhjqD6tVDvdWmZ+o8I\nJsDcXEMAhHKclPnuh9TrAQLWHMr8MDNyHZT6BkMW2DjogMqOKU29WPmARQFNwIa9\nppWtWdzk/wM1zoOTRm3co69ym7tdhgeHP6JXVktJ/AyI8rRpMGqzsuVlST1gRmOJ\nZBakHXyHQS9J0ouvpp0mauBuRiyU6DDZQaNm1CBz6g18d0p/8cG7M5YJqLXoepb6\nFUfVlPFAXr57+5aDs5e2oNBXxuCLyAu8qMQ3Mu5WImN7sFJ92iusCWM/8eZuCH3I\nn82TR6rjuGpcFjzaHaM0VR00tcZipD94VJ+p2r79FK7ynoHHvWeSR3wDLDQUQe+W\nlJ0tYje5TW+trldAkGLvDa0pLj1Kz/swEjl2cU7JN4fVZQzcfxWE78nPfPPU+EwQ\nLkQyvqBdxF/T0IiVsFpr2eq6RU6izpEApVn2QPEjv3GHkeDM1JUhP1FYy6uk40nu\n71ewYDpRaRQ4BxqqS3f6gMN0EOpdbwLHw+l3yp3hXiCougY19d5dDuxJvbGEwGxy\n9iufbeY7oQyttcl+tnjygS0E/vXWUrkzXu8uLjEp3mq6L3ZhY5NYxW6I5YjU0tB2\nbRWlGSDLHnHccXGjgkPlQduUK19zMgjcHgICc2s7N5xKXq/1gM+WYrXt0XeiHxJo\ntW/2EYVG/8FWVZpAEh1V\n=dLvS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T22:58:13Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/u