fter teardown the behaviour is: `INDEX` is never in the switcher and is\nreachable only by an explicit deep link or direct URL; the duplicate is\nthe landing view and behaves like any user-created view. If the user\ndeletes every view on an object, `INDEX` shows again — that is a\nview-count fallback and needs no flag.\n\n### Where the filter lives\n\n- `ViewService.getFilteredFlatViews` — the choke point behind the\ncollection reads.\n- `MinimalMetadataService` — the bootstrap views payload, which does not\ngo through `ViewService`.\n\nBoth apply `isInitialObjectView`, a pure per-row predicate: a view is\nthe initial one when its own `universalIdentifier` equals the identifier\nderived from its own application and object. No application lookup, no\nobject-map recompute, nothing that can throw, and the cost is one hash\nper view only while the flag is off.\n\nReads by id (`findById`, `findByIdWithRelations`) are deliberately\n**not** filtered. The row id is a `v4`, not the deterministic\nidentifier, so it is not guessable, and the flag exists to control what\nthe picker shows rather than to be an access-control boundary.\n\nThe write side is not gated by the flag either: the view is created\nwhenever an object is created, and simply stays hidden until the flag is\non.\n\nAn earlier revision used a `ViewKey.DEFAULT` marker instead; it was\ndropped because it put a transitional value in a public enum and\n`CreateViewInput` let any API caller claim it. A later revision resolved\nthe hidden set through a service that looked up the workspace-custom\napplication on every read; that was replaced by the per-row predicate\nabove, which cannot throw on a workspace whose application registration\nis missing.\n\n## Also not addressed\n\n- **Application-owned objects installed after the backfill.** The\nbackfill seeds every existing non-remote object with a live `INDEX`\nview, app-owned ones included, so they get a user-owned view. But the\ncreate-time handler only fires for workspace-custom objects, so an app\nobject installed later gets none. Closing that means letting an\napplication sync emit workspace-custom rows, which stops the sync being\na pure projection of its manifest - a design call, and a blocker for the\nteardown step above.\n- **New fields do not append to the initial view.** That follows from\nthe ownership decision and is intended, but it is worth re-litigating: a\nnew custom field is invisible in the view people use until they add the\ncolumn.\n- **View ordering.** Felix noted Opportunity ends up with two same-level\nviews and Kanban should sort first. The initial view takes a fixed\nposition, which keeps it out of first place, but nothing reorders\nexisting views.\n- **The initial view name is a plain English literal**, while\n`INDEX_VIEW_NAME` is an i18n template resolved at read time. A\nnon-English workspace shows the two side by side in different languages,\nand an object rename does not propagate to the initial view.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `twenty-server` and `twenty-shared` typecheck clean; oxlint and oxfmt\nclean.\n- Unit specs for the deterministic identifiers, the initial view shape,\nand the initial field set (caller field included, label identifier at\nposition 0, user-owned keying).\n- Backfill run against a real workspace: 63 initial views and their\nfields created, exactly one per object, no duplicates, and invisible in\nthe UI while the flag was off.\n- Browser pass with the flag on: the initial view becomes the landing\nview, `INDEX` drops out of the switcher, a deep link to the `INDEX` view\nid still renders it, and deleting the initial view falls back to\n`INDEX`.\n- Not run locally: the integration suites.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqqp1eCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA3q0QAGreqcjuxBM8uCz9U7vFv2L7\n1XCZv+UmshvoU5/WaiUupzLFmF6tZBC7R4BZMiJwuQqa1oPrphrzFgPn2rmsJo4I\nlANeRXMukLyfZvl42qITaVyiSOZv1UzBqgXQqk8b21Ftj0hkhhZk+wrvBJr2tdtK\nm1MnJf8GFVsKiVqRUvA4L/AwRUmf5Xk8Dr5XdBBY08