56cd0e24e0ff402"}],"sha":"09fd8e98e52ef9d2baa3ef4abf97e1b6822cb064","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/09fd8e98e52ef9d2baa3ef4abf97e1b6822cb064"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/6cf8812f15295e41b75c1197456cd0e24e0ff402/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T02:21:30Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T02:21:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: survive the first-dial reset instead of crash-looping on boot (#7)\n\nDeploying #6 to the kind cluster produced CrashLoopBackOff. Two defects,\nboth mine, both invisible until a real rollout:\n\n1. wireOrders dialled Postgres ONCE. In this cluster every injected pod''s\n   first outbound TCP dial is reset ~10s after the app starts (Istio native\n   sidecars; holdApplicationUntilProxyStarts is a no-op for them) -- a\n   known, documented, transient condition here. One attempt turns it into a\n   fatal boot error: \"run migrations: failed to open database: read tcp\n   ...: read: connection reset by peer\".\n\n2. The health server starts AFTER the database work, so while migrations\n   run nothing is listening. The liveness probe got a 500 from the sidecar\n   and the kubelet killed the pod -- exit code 143, SIGTERM, a pod murdered\n   mid-boot. That exit code is what distinguished this from an app crash,\n   and it is why the previous pod survived: its migrations were already\n   applied and returned instantly.\n\nFix 1: retry with exponential backoff, 5 attempts over ~31s, around both\nthe migration and the ping. This does NOT weaken the fail-closed rule from\n#5 -- after the budget it still refuses to boot, and it still reports the\nLAST real error rather than a generic timeout, so a wrong password still\nsays so. It only stops treating a sidecar warm-up as permanent.\n\nFix 2: a startupProbe (30 x 2s = 60s) defers liveness until the process is\nactually serving. Liveness is unchanged and still takes over afterwards, so\na genuinely broken database still fails the pod -- it is just no longer\nmistaken for a slow one.\n\nTests, and one that did not work at first: retry_test.go covers the helper\n(recovers on attempt 2, no sleep on first success, gives up reporting the\nreal cause, honours cancellation, budget outlasts the 10s reset). Reverting\nwireOrders to the direct call did NOT fail any of them -- proving the\nhelper works is not the same claim as proving wireOrders USES it, which is\nexactly the defect that shipped. Added wiring_test.go, which drives\nwireOrders at an unreachable port and asserts on elapsed time; it fails in\n1.2ms against the reverted version. The chart test now also asserts the\nstartupProbe exists and outlasts the retry budget.\n\nAll 4 code mutants and the chart mutant verified to fail. Base delay is\ninjectable so the give-up test does not sleep out 31s (suite went 17s ->\n1.3s).\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0%, integration green, both\nchart suites green.","tree":{"sha":"b58a6f59351ab7d28d10194ddb0747d53c61d36c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/b58a6f59351ab7d28d10194ddb0747d53c61d36c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/6cf8812f15295e41b75c1197456cd0e24e0ff402","verification":{"payload":"tree b58a6f59351ab7d28d10194ddb0747d53c61d36c\nparent 479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790216490 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790216490 -0300\n\nfix: survive the first-dial reset instead of crash-looping on boot (#7)\n\nDeploying #6 to the kind cluster produced CrashLoopBackOff. Two defects,\nboth mine, both invisible until a real rollout:\n\n1. wireOrders dialled Postgres ONCE. In this cluster every injected pod''s\n   first outbound TCP dial is reset ~10s after the app starts (Istio native\n   sidecars; holdApplicationUntilProxyStarts is a no-op for them) -- a\n   known, documented, transient condition here. One attempt turns it into a\n   fatal boot error: \"run migrations: failed to open database: read tcp\n   ...: read: connection reset by peer\".\n\n2. The health server starts AFTER the database work, so while migrations\n   run nothing is listening. The liveness probe got a 500 from the sidecar\n   and the kubelet killed the pod -- exit code 143, SIGTERM, a pod murdered\n   mid-boot. That exit code is what distinguished this from an app crash,\n   and it is why the previous pod survived: its migrations were already\n   applied and returned instantly.\n\nFix 1: retry with exponential backoff, 5 attempts over ~31s, around both\nthe migration and the ping. This does NOT weaken the fail-closed rule from\n#5 -- after the budget it still refuses to boot, and it still reports the\nLAST real error rather than a generic timeout, so a wrong password still\nsays so. It only stops treating a sidecar warm-up as permanent.\n\nFix 2: a startupProbe (30 x 2s = 60s) defers liveness until the process is\nactually serving. Liveness is unchanged and still takes over afterwards, so\na genuinely broken database still fails the pod -- it is just no longer\nmistaken for a slow one.\n\nTests, and one that did not work at first: retry_test.go covers the helper\n(recovers on attempt 2, no sleep on first success, gives up reporting the\nreal cause, honours cancellation, budget outlasts the 10s reset). Reverting\nwireOrders to the direct call did NOT fail any of them -- proving the\nhelper works is not the same claim as proving wireOrders USES it, which is\nexactly the defect that shipped. Added wiring_test.go, which drives\nwireOrders at an unreachable port and asserts on elapsed time; it fails in\n1.2ms against the reverted version. The chart test now also asserts the\nstartupProbe exists and outlasts the retry budget.\n\nAll 4 code mutants and the chart mutant verified to fail. Base delay is\ninjectable so the give-up test does not sleep out 31s (suite went 17s ->\n1.3s).\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0%, integration green, both\nchart suites green.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtIkqCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANH4QAC7yCub1pcpw19696Hth37hA\nzx3xwzz8HAdaWgyJGgJf4MHGG2IRShtDhqsgsS40X+IIdJ9Huds0DTAV5SFm1A8G\nq8QUtsxNbmetAzymIi5uTF/RlWxuxlAKyxmnaiVER9InuOGruDxCf0PmUnKbzobe\nV5R/lfe2ElIBIMXE9VQtvhMsocNuiZJAHAi0d3/qWfwDPNRhPDseZxyFbYXWuTtQ\nD3s8DwhkcChlPvHnZkGxRqDbNNph2q+bjjc85tPW92DKnbNX4/221hZpeXPxPRI7\nZ+wE1JQNfSyf33SamBxwPeJu+iK1hlciRXCNfZvt5Q8OedgDgoWHiJcOJpX8z1vE\nqvPX2Bo0ej1HoCPb25ETWZjj1jAqKU8Pud6y33lm6RLNwp6FIVPX7TEF8ZlqTBgm\n/ogw+hKZvgwgFDkY+nxFoX3QfksuLglA2wvQbrwfcMmfaO0svIvT01CJUEkoZUJO\nAIBfAb1pMyIjN9QCqZ4e1AlpY46DOWkLQSqYx3uVW7b4dmTNIUBJ/T35+HK0bH9T\n2foYv3v/d8GiQvCL5n6+6M99t4dqV34PTxYzgsb7WD+//mv+hZHlT4JTXeNhPABh\n7cL3bfHpSnLziJ4LW32O67tcTgA0LiikeP2PuVidLORgBMk0VwAw9Byv/2dDbLd6\n4eMpHbRtRf+WdxdYSMFo\n=PrnU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T02:21:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/6cf8812f15295e41b75c1197456cd0e24e0ff402","node_id":"C_kwDOUmckp9oAKDZjZjg4MTJmMTUyOTVlNDFiNzVjMTE5NzQ1NmNkMGUyNGUwZmY0MDI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235","sha":"479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235"}],"sha":"6cf8812f15295e41b75c1197456cd0e24e0ff402","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/6cf8812f15295e41b75c1197456cd0e24e0ff402"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T02:01:41Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T02:01:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: wire the inbound leg — poller, read-only REST, curated ACL files (#6)\n\nReceiveNetworkDemand was constructed and then DISCARDED (`_ = receive`).\nNothing in a deployed environment could create a NetworkOrder at all: the\naggregate, its persistence and its sweep were all real, and the one thing\nthat produces work for them was dead code.\n\nCorrection to an earlier claim of mine: I described intake as \"reachable\nonly from the poll loop\". There was no poll loop.\n\nINBOUND POLLER (internal/adapters/inbound/poller). The network offers no\npush (ADR 0001 section 5), so this is the whole inbound path. Rules worth\nnaming, each pinned by a test:\n\n- A failed unit does NOT advance the watermark, so the demand is\n  re-fetched. Processing is idempotent on networkRef, which is what makes\n  the retry safe; advancing past an order whose 24h clock is running loses\n  it silently.\n- The watermark is read BEFORE the gateway call, not after. Taken\n  afterwards it skips demand the network accepted while we were waiting.\n- One bad order does not abort the pass. The others are real demand with\n  their own clocks running.\n- Run polls IMMEDIATELY rather than sleeping out the first interval: a\n  restart is when we are most likely to be behind, and a poller outage is\n  an SLA breach, not a delayed batch.\n- An empty poll still advances, or a quiet period makes the window grow\n  forever.\n\nREAD-ONLY REST (internal/adapters/inbound/http, apis/openapi.yaml). No\nwrite endpoint, deliberately: an HTTP intake would be a second fictional\ninbound path, and the only thing it could honestly do is inject test\ndemand. What is exposed is observation — what we told the network about a\npurchase order, and whether the poller is alive. The latter matters\nbecause a stalled poller is SILENT: no orders, no errors, a healthy\n/healthz, and a window closing regardless.\n\nThe DTO carries no customer PII (ADR 0001 hard rule 2) and a test asserts\nthat against a list of field names, since the aggregate will later hold\nship-to detail the outbound legs need. Lines carry BOTH vocabularies, or\nthe ACL''s translation decision is invisible to whoever reconciles a\ndisputed order. Overdue is derived via the domain predicate the sweep\nuses, so the surface cannot disagree with the sweep.\n\nerrors.go mirrors order-management''s two-table RFC 7807 mapping, and\nerrors_test.go enforces the pairing structurally — that is exactly the\ndefect OM shipped (a 409 delivered as type=internal-error) and it is\ncheap to prevent rather than rediscover.\n\nTHE GAP THE SMOKE TEST FOUND. With the poller wired, a real run rejected\nits seeded order as untranslatable: the ProductTranslation dictionary is\nempty in every deployment, so 100% of demand would be refused. The inbound\nleg would have been alive and useless, and invisibly so, because refusing\nunknown products is ALSO correct behaviour. Added\nPRODUCT_TRANSLATION_FILE, a curated JSON dictionary, with a startup WARNING\nwhen it is absent. A validated-before-applied load, so a bad entry cannot\nleave a half-loaded dictionary that translates some lines of an order and\nrejects others — the protocol has no way to express that.\n\nNETWORK_SEED_FILE does the same for stub demand and refuses to boot if set\nagainst a non-stub gateway, because seeded demand would never be delivered\nand somebody would be waiting for it. Relative deadlines (\"+36h\") are\nsupported because a fixed instant in a committed fixture goes stale and\nevery order becomes instantly infeasible, which reads like a promise bug.\n\nChart: POLL_INTERVAL, plus both files rendered into a ConfigMap and\nmounted. tests/test_data_files.py PARSES the rendered JSON rather than\ngrepping it — the templates build JSON with range loops and commas, and a\nmalformed file mounts fine and fails at startup looking like a code bug.\nIt also resolves config the way Kubernetes does (envFrom + inline env\noverride); reading only inline env reported POLL_INTERVAL missing when\nenvFrom already delivered it.\n\nCI: api-lint added (spectral, pinned via apis/tooling). Both chart suites\nnow run on every PR — process-path-management has a render-assertion\nscript that is not wired to CI and only runs if somebody remembers.\n\nLint caught a real defect: the product-translation load sat after\n`defer closeOrders()`, so its os.Exit(1) leaked the pool. Moved above the\ndatabase wiring, where no os.Exit can outrun a defer. gosec G304 on the\ntwo config reads uses the same justified nolint the fleet already uses for\noperator-controlled paths.\n\nVerified end to end against the REAL deployed order-management: seeded\ndemand was polled, translated (ASIN-1 -> sku-1), a held order was raised,\nand OM answered not-feasible, so NF rejected it. That is the FeasibleBy\nverdict crossing both contexts for the first time. All 5 poller mutants,\n5 HTTP mutants and 4 chart mutants verified to fail.\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0% (gate 90), mutation 100%\nefficacy, spectral clean.","tree":{"sha":"e3ad10a4d026a532d62f853e756f7e2abb079b6b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/e3ad10a4d026a532d62f853e756f7e2abb079b6b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235","verification":{"payload":"tree e3ad10a4d026a532d62f853e756f7e2abb079b6b\nparent 7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790215301 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790215301 -0300\n\nfeat: wire the inbound leg — poller, read-only REST, curated ACL files (#6)\n\nReceiveNetworkDemand was constructed and then DISCARDED (`_ = receive`).\nNothing in a deployed environment could create a NetworkOrder at all: the\naggregate, its persistence and its sweep were all real, and the one thing\nthat produces work for them was dead code.\n\nCorrection to an earlier claim of mine: I described intake as \"reachable\nonly from the poll loop\". There was no poll loop.\n\nINBOUND POLLER (internal/adapters/inbound/poller). The network offers no\npush (ADR 0001 section 5), so this is the whole inbound path. Rules worth\nnaming, each pinned by a test:\n\n- A failed unit does NOT advance the watermark, so the demand is\n  re-fetched. Processing is idempotent on networkRef, which is what makes\n  the retry safe; advancing past an order whose 24h clock is running loses\n  it silently.\n- The watermark is read BEFORE the gateway call, not after. Taken\n  afterwards it skips demand the network accepted while we were waiting.\n- One bad order does not abort the pass. The others are real demand with\n  their own clocks running.\n- Run polls IMMEDIATELY rather than sleeping out the first interval: a\n  restart is when we are most likely to be behind, and a poller outage is\n  an SLA breach, not a delayed batch.\n- An empty poll still advances, or a quiet period makes the window grow\n  forever.\n\nREAD-ONLY REST (internal/adapters/inbound/http, apis/openapi.yaml). No\nwrite endpoint, deliberately: an HTTP intake would be a second fictional\ninbound path, and the only thing it could honestly do is inject test\ndemand. What is exposed is observation — what we told the network about a\npurchase order, and whether the poller is alive. The latter matters\nbecause a stalled poller is SILENT: no orders, no errors, a healthy\n/healthz, and a window closing regardless.\n\nThe DTO carries no customer PII (ADR 0001 hard rule 2) and a test asserts\nthat against a list of field names, since the aggregate will later hold\nship-to detail the outbound legs need. Lines carry BOTH vocabularies, or\nthe ACL''s translation decision is invisible to whoever reconciles a\ndisputed order. Overdue is derived via the domain predicate the sweep\nuses, so the surface cannot disagree with the sweep.\n\nerrors.go mirrors order-management''s two-table RFC 7807 mapping, and\nerrors_test.go enforces the pairing structurally — that is exactly the\ndefect OM shipped (a 409 delivered as type=internal-error) and it is\ncheap to prevent rather than rediscover.\n\nTHE GAP THE SMOKE TEST FOUND. With the poller wired, a real run rejected\nits seeded order as untranslatable: the ProductTranslation dictionary is\nempty in every deployment, so 100% of demand would be refused. The inbound\nleg would have been alive and useless, and invisibly so, because refusing\nunknown products is ALSO correct behaviour. Added\nPRODUCT_TRANSLATION_FILE, a curated JSON dictionary, with a startup WARNING\nwhen it is absent. A validated-before-applied load, so a bad entry cannot\nleave a half-loaded dictionary that translates some lines of an order and\nrejects others — the protocol has no way to express that.\n\nNETWORK_SEED_FILE does the same for stub demand and refuses to boot if set\nagainst a non-stub gateway, because seeded demand would never be delivered\nand somebody would be waiting for it. Relative deadlines (\"+36h\") are\nsupported because a fixed instant in a committed fixture goes stale and\nevery order becomes instantly infeasible, which reads like a promise bug.\n\nChart: POLL_INTERVAL, plus both files rendered into a ConfigMap and\nmounted. tests/test_data_files.py PARSES the rendered JSON rather than\ngrepping it — the templates build JSON with range loops and commas, and a\nmalformed file mounts fine and fails at startup looking like a code bug.\nIt also resolves config the way Kubernetes does (envFrom + inline env\noverride); reading only inline env reported POLL_INTERVAL missing when\nenvFrom already delivered it.\n\nCI: api-lint added (spectral, pinned via apis/tooling). Both chart suites\nnow run on every PR — process-path-management has a render-assertion\nscript that is not wired to CI and only runs if somebody remembers.\n\nLint caught a real defect: the product-translation load sat after\n`defer closeOrders()`, so its os.Exit(1) leaked the pool. Moved above the\ndatabase wiring, where no os.Exit can outrun a defer. gosec G304 on the\ntwo config reads uses the same justified nolint the fleet already uses for\noperator-controlled paths.\n\nVerified end to end against the REAL deployed order-management: seeded\ndemand was polled, translated (ASIN-1 -> sku-1), a held order was raised,\nand OM answered not-feasible, so NF rejected it. That is the FeasibleBy\nverdict crossing both contexts for the first time. All 5 poller mutants,\n5 HTTP mutants and 4 chart mutants verified to fail.\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0% (gate 90), mutation 100%\nefficacy, spectral clean.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtISFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAlI4QAH7sq3Vs5jtq61z/BBKJhUJi\nttTvmCXVxI7LMIqRvensRo5Vc6BStcJDZtN5QpwoXc3qabA4SSqsQNshKf2t6jEX\nDIym6cpxe4A6q84gSGTdNcPRpyn7IYfRSoYvjiQ/CEM+qaq3GxOoXI1h90mobijC\nj2KIEbLnMnzNnZNZ/XYp5AsDn82I5mluAGi1UpqWPbaMQZX9+Oj//N4bNc489EyA\n+S8qwQHepqW8bAaoPWbpVWTKfuf6PR52W8/K/dsMuHGEBVT1Xreuzi37Eh3H/Kmn\nMxrNUEGP/iPIGy/RSaNBruyz64yakGuNZoe+tds861Ofgvm0fWEDkK34ceyW5r/D\nQxldP1PBfVaFtU0OgpT5cOyqMyuSZGm0DRyW8vBQR8UXyeCgYP6y6arVEkAR0jQ0\noeXv65bA0FYrmFtf4trvYmhFrO4b3aoLNTiXyFPuGq5O4e6jW9lM5mzWqgmAyM2E\ncf6Wc4UGzxbOyMx1vbDdbVY1A1qfaYfojYOB0outIURE0O8Msm0oqTfLWRkHG/bR\nNp6U3UlVQjV13n5qNrvMPuk2+4YdAAZ9HV0q2+nf8pTPplFa3/fOU+egUlyh7tGN\nCn95N6MwlB5LXtX9mwyT9guqmYbjgSWfrNkO3INQnvwGrRX9DbyK3+OrQ0qMTARl\nsgl2+UXQRTLZFieIYKJ7\n=U1Wc\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T02:01:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235","node_id":"C_kwDOUmckp9oAKDQ3OWI0MWJhMjc1MDJiMjIzMDQ3MTdjNzk2YWJlNDllMjdiYTAyMzU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c","sha":"7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c"}],"sha":"479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/479b41ba27502b22304717c796abe49e27ba0235"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-24T01:10:24Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-24T01:10:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: Postgres persistence for NetworkOrder (ADR 0001) (#5)\n\nThe aggregate carried the 24h acknowledgement deadlines the sweep\nenforces, in a map. Pods in this fleet restart once at rollout by design,\nso every deadline this context owed the network was forgotten on a\nschedule, and the sweep loop ran faithfully over an empty repo.\n\nSchema (migrations/0001): network_orders + network_order_lines. Three\nchoices worth naming:\n\n- network_ref is the PRIMARY KEY, not a surrogate id. This context is\n  Conformist upstream and the network addresses every acknowledgement by\n  that reference; a second identity would be a second source of truth.\n- acknowledge_by is PERSISTED, not recomputed on read, and the upsert\n  deliberately excludes it (and received_at). The inbound leg is a poll,\n  so the same purchase order can arrive twice and ReceiveNetworkDemand\n  anchors a fresh window to the LATER arrival -- accepting that value\n  would reset a clock already running and make an overdue order look new.\n- No \"must have at least one line\" rule. An untranslatable order is\n  lineless by construction (ReceiveUntranslatable) and exists precisely so\n  the refusal is recorded rather than the demand dropped. This is also why\n  the order row is read separately from its lines: a join would make that\n  legitimate order indistinguishable from one that does not exist.\n\nComposition root: DATABASE_URL unset keeps the in-memory repo (zero-config\nrun, hermetic unit suite); set means Postgres or a refusal to boot, with a\nPing so a misconfigured deployment fails at startup instead of inside the\nfirst request. Never a silent fallback -- that is the failure whose cost is\nthe deadlines above. Migrations run at startup and now ship in the image.\n\nChart: database.url / existingSecret, following order-management exactly.\nThe default render still has no DATABASE_URL and creates no Secret, which\nis what keeps a no-values install working against a cluster with no\nPostgres for this context.\n\nCI: `integration` job added, testcontainers-started Postgres inside the\ntest -- never a DATABASE_URL service container with a skip gate, which\nwould report success while asserting nothing. It also builds and vets under\nthe integration tag, since those files are invisible to the default build.\n\nTests: 6 integration tests over a real Postgres 16. Verified all six\ndiscriminate by hand-building mutants -- rewriting acknowledge_by on\nupsert, dropping local_order_id, ignoring state in ListUnanswered,\ndropping findLines, turning ErrNoRows into an error, losing the network\nproduct id: each fails at least one test. The deadline mutant SURVIVED the\nfirst version of that test, which re-saved an aggregate already agreeing\nwith the row; rewritten to re-deliver the same PO with a later arrival,\nwhich is the real scenario, it now fails. Chart test extended to cover all\nfour database paths; both its new mutants verified.\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0% (gate 90), mutation 100%\nefficacy / 93.33% coverage unchanged.","tree":{"sha":"3b92dca09905d368192125667bd3e364bb4c6a55","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/3b92dca09905d368192125667bd3e364bb4c6a55"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c","verification":{"payload":"tree 3b92dca09905d368192125667bd3e364bb4c6a55\nparent 0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790212224 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790212224 -0300\n\nfeat: Postgres persistence for NetworkOrder (ADR 0001) (#5)\n\nThe aggregate carried the 24h acknowledgement deadlines the sweep\nenforces, in a map. Pods in this fleet restart once at rollout by design,\nso every deadline this context owed the network was forgotten on a\nschedule, and the sweep loop ran faithfully over an empty repo.\n\nSchema (migrations/0001): network_orders + network_order_lines. Three\nchoices worth naming:\n\n- network_ref is the PRIMARY KEY, not a surrogate id. This context is\n  Conformist upstream and the network addresses every acknowledgement by\n  that reference; a second identity would be a second source of truth.\n- acknowledge_by is PERSISTED, not recomputed on read, and the upsert\n  deliberately excludes it (and received_at). The inbound leg is a poll,\n  so the same purchase order can arrive twice and ReceiveNetworkDemand\n  anchors a fresh window to the LATER arrival -- accepting that value\n  would reset a clock already running and make an overdue order look new.\n- No \"must have at least one line\" rule. An untranslatable order is\n  lineless by construction (ReceiveUntranslatable) and exists precisely so\n  the refusal is recorded rather than the demand dropped. This is also why\n  the order row is read separately from its lines: a join would make that\n  legitimate order indistinguishable from one that does not exist.\n\nComposition root: DATABASE_URL unset keeps the in-memory repo (zero-config\nrun, hermetic unit suite); set means Postgres or a refusal to boot, with a\nPing so a misconfigured deployment fails at startup instead of inside the\nfirst request. Never a silent fallback -- that is the failure whose cost is\nthe deadlines above. Migrations run at startup and now ship in the image.\n\nChart: database.url / existingSecret, following order-management exactly.\nThe default render still has no DATABASE_URL and creates no Secret, which\nis what keeps a no-values install working against a cluster with no\nPostgres for this context.\n\nCI: `integration` job added, testcontainers-started Postgres inside the\ntest -- never a DATABASE_URL service container with a skip gate, which\nwould report success while asserting nothing. It also builds and vets under\nthe integration tag, since those files are invisible to the default build.\n\nTests: 6 integration tests over a real Postgres 16. Verified all six\ndiscriminate by hand-building mutants -- rewriting acknowledge_by on\nupsert, dropping local_order_id, ignoring state in ListUnanswered,\ndropping findLines, turning ErrNoRows into an error, losing the network\nproduct id: each fails at least one test. The deadline mutant SURVIVED the\nfirst version of that test, which re-saved an aggregate already agreeing\nwith the row; rewritten to re-deliver the same PO with a later arrival,\nwhich is the real scenario, it now fails. Chart test extended to cover all\nfour database paths; both its new mutants verified.\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0% (gate 90), mutation 100%\nefficacy / 93.33% coverage unchanged.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtHiACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAAwYQAKlVUoStjhUo71hkS72x38Uc\nbUCAwbIMoOHiNhoqF9Li/3UfEjTptDChEsnNFn07FGFYxGAhTHF80TTIqGpt23Ul\njsZjqXQfMGCYzPFFxG7+e2tI6+lLFA+1Kxk1xGUb9QxccmG0Iy6Etv19groYd4Dl\nASrGJOjy6wGhe7ExUkUzwmn0Wo/EQsBHhKdercEDSaJpeiJvSEh4xd+jbnJ+7UQD\nbDjcBCcRjX+lB1x4pqrhm3SBTlFL4rTVulD6HGnc1NriQt6DFveEqHh5DEQBsA7/\nsCBgv5mWuYSuXUSSMAOAXfroD8VwLqAG728vVvDovsU0ZYPhlrzP3NgXt4oZTI71\nWj7qN62wMWaD4atNLlv/cCwM6Wgl0518/CjCRBki0Lxtegk6/eau3m+y/7RvdxgZ\nfxPjOeW8Ltq/ZAU5RkUKW0/jlEFJIeP3CiQAfsqy+JpYmWc2m/OVBpTGNwDx4naQ\n6GNG1j9B+T+4an3PEWWT/uBuRKXUOPJ7OyCtF6lRru4YJiQmbP0RyHdV6lIEMAsu\nD77SG0WtWoaL+YNu1yT8Hy2AbZSt5kxezEcsh3Fh5sBsLMfm1g0u/qqAHyH5VkkJ\nMh/dk8W5+Ix1m5ljXUBnv35ZvPQu7BtRFfVPyDcGhvWjoytAu/vA0Do+gOW9Qlg0\nAlZjALmLPD4zgsDmNZRm\n=R80K\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-24T01:10:24Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c","node_id":"C_kwDOUmckp9oAKDc5NThjMzAwODIyN2Y5OGY3ODhlYzZkZDFlYmNiNTk1Y2Y3OTA5NGM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c","sha":"0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c"}],"sha":"7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/7958c3008227f98f788ec6dd1ebcb595cf79094c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T02:34:27Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T02:34:27Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: Dockerfile and Helm chart, with a fail-closed credential guard (#4)\n\nCloses the deploy blocker: warehouse-infra could not register this context\nbecause there was no chart_path to point at, and no image to run.\n\nDockerfile mirrors the fleet''s two-stage build. Two deliberate\ndifferences from the siblings:\n- golang:1.27-alpine, because this repo''s go.mod is 1.27 while most of\n  the fleet is 1.26. The digest was resolved from the registry, not\n  copied from a sibling whose tag points at a different minor.\n- no migrations/ COPY, because this context persists nothing yet.\n\nca-certificates is not the boilerplate it is elsewhere: this context''s\nwhole job is calling an external network over TLS, and without a trust\nstore that fails at runtime rather than at build time.\n\nCHART BUG FOUND AND FIXED WHILE WRITING THE TESTS. The first draft\nrendered the three NETWORK_* secretKeyRefs whenever networkMode was not\n\"stub\", but only created the Secret when inline credentials were\nsupplied. Asking for sandbox without credentials therefore produced a\nDeployment referencing a Secret that does not exist -- Kubernetes leaves\nthat pod in CreateContainerConfigError, which looks like a cluster\nproblem rather than the binary''s own clear fail-closed message. The\nchart now fails at TEMPLATE time with a reason.\n\ncharts/network-fulfillment/tests/test_credential_wiring.py asserts all\nfour paths: stub creates and references nothing, sandbox/live wire every\nreferenced Secret, existingSecret is trusted and never overwritten, and\nnon-stub without credentials fails closed. Verified it discriminates by\nremoving the guard -- the test fails.\n\nUnlike the fleet''s main-only helm-lint convention, the new helm-lint job\nruns on EVERY PR: a credential-wiring regression that only surfaced on a\nmain-targeting PR would already be merged to develop by then.\n\nVerified: helm lint clean, all four render paths checked by hand, the\nexact Dockerfile build command compiles, and a native build of the same\nsource honours PORT and serves /healthz 200 in stub mode with no\nconfiguration at all. The image itself could not be built here (no\nDocker daemon on this machine) -- CI''s trivy-scan job is the first real\nbuild.","tree":{"sha":"bd8f96a92b1f74d674fc82e21e5f7dc57655e4fa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/bd8f96a92b1f74d674fc82e21e5f7dc57655e4fa"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c","verification":{"payload":"tree bd8f96a92b1f74d674fc82e21e5f7dc57655e4fa\nparent f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790130867 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790130867 -0300\n\nfeat: Dockerfile and Helm chart, with a fail-closed credential guard (#4)\n\nCloses the deploy blocker: warehouse-infra could not register this context\nbecause there was no chart_path to point at, and no image to run.\n\nDockerfile mirrors the fleet''s two-stage build. Two deliberate\ndifferences from the siblings:\n- golang:1.27-alpine, because this repo''s go.mod is 1.27 while most of\n  the fleet is 1.26. The digest was resolved from the registry, not\n  copied from a sibling whose tag points at a different minor.\n- no migrations/ COPY, because this context persists nothing yet.\n\nca-certificates is not the boilerplate it is elsewhere: this context''s\nwhole job is calling an external network over TLS, and without a trust\nstore that fails at runtime rather than at build time.\n\nCHART BUG FOUND AND FIXED WHILE WRITING THE TESTS. The first draft\nrendered the three NETWORK_* secretKeyRefs whenever networkMode was not\n\"stub\", but only created the Secret when inline credentials were\nsupplied. Asking for sandbox without credentials therefore produced a\nDeployment referencing a Secret that does not exist -- Kubernetes leaves\nthat pod in CreateContainerConfigError, which looks like a cluster\nproblem rather than the binary''s own clear fail-closed message. The\nchart now fails at TEMPLATE time with a reason.\n\ncharts/network-fulfillment/tests/test_credential_wiring.py asserts all\nfour paths: stub creates and references nothing, sandbox/live wire every\nreferenced Secret, existingSecret is trusted and never overwritten, and\nnon-stub without credentials fails closed. Verified it discriminates by\nremoving the guard -- the test fails.\n\nUnlike the fleet''s main-only helm-lint convention, the new helm-lint job\nruns on EVERY PR: a credential-wiring regression that only surfaced on a\nmain-targeting PR would already be merged to develop by then.\n\nVerified: helm lint clean, all four render paths checked by hand, the\nexact Dockerfile build command compiles, and a native build of the same\nsource honours PORT and serves /healthz 200 in stub mode with no\nconfiguration at all. The image itself could not be built here (no\nDocker daemon on this machine) -- CI''s trivy-scan job is the first real\nbuild.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqszqzCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAADOkQAJSNtqw8Ymz5dhO9E8AQNRr7\nx23dCXE6Q00vHM95a1iBJPCKzTpfsFg847t1OZPG/0MYoVxyBMAroGKpXp6uqy4e\n55Kfe9nU4+otjAayiYYQZ20nrYLTwz/7NWbIN1srqZzXdSClf6xO759iVd6OiFlS\nw+ESDSLKN/f0L2UrI3qdDVKefhsA8XsI9zrVHn33uVDcXSxOI3/KAMuFE/OTcl/s\nvIJ/OGi0iXnBSbcWRqLsDb3m2qdMRknz8K425BOh7s1mE5MtorMfuVNXRJkFvz16\neFVe2fHpXkvZLnXRvIScva4crb6NWaAMKgaRIDOyQNAgN73rUpxPsB9lXieziE1q\nyAnnU3fzKDEo8I/zuGBq5ahT+36mF7LixEVWjyGoBEexIj92V0hztIlHiwewVVA/\nHtdyGjpfWwiWpkJn1oiQFESmA+C26BHvR5MpKNmuyLx9jht9K0X6y+ABUIU4Pf2i\nuHY1NN2h6j6cDS5bIgFRLEov89LnDi4j/7pOAVcGxC3Cyx20KiXMYQdfhBv7a4cR\npG6rckx4HbTVaJx5sjvR4p7UOOfzXg4y4u9LRviJL+zPol4lVTKWZjeypfIDSl5g\nOTG8bqTHJeC377qr51mytjeeObkcrExY6R3P3mg27qoG6J+JNpaQe+mNpG7gFsWL\nklCFIWOeoVKSn8my8KNy\n=Cr3H\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T02:34:27Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c","node_id":"C_kwDOUmckp9oAKDBmYWU4NGNjZTgwZTMxODY1ODVmOTRkMDRmNzBkZGYzNjVjZmQxNWM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88","sha":"f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88"}],"sha":"0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/0fae84cce80e3186585f94d04f70ddf365cfd15c"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T02:18:17Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T02:18:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(planner): send requiredShipBy and let order-management judge feasibility (#3)\n\nCloses the HONEST LIMITATION documented in #2. order-management now\naccepts requiredShipBy on intake (its ADR 0020 phase 4, PR #83), so this\nadapter sends the network''s deadline and takes the verdict rather than\ncomputing its own.\n\nBefore: we sent no deadline, order-management returned the EARLIEST\npromise it could make, and this adapter compared two instants. That\nworked, but it reimplemented the rule here and produced the\nearliest-window answer rather than FeasibleBy''s latest-window one --\ngiving up the floor slack ADR 0020 §2 exists to preserve.\n\nAfter: the deadline goes in the request. A promise back means\norder-management committed to a departure at or before it; no promise\nmeans it could not. An absent promiseDate is now a VERDICT, not a\nmissing field -- for an ordinary order a null promise means \"not\ncomputed yet\", but for an order carrying a deadline it means \"we cannot\nmeet it\".\n\nWe deliberately do NOT re-check the returned date against the deadline.\nA second opinion computed here could only ever disagree with the\nauthority, and disagreeing with the authority is how two services end up\npromising different things. The test table asserts exactly this: a\npromise dated after our deadline still reads feasible.\n\nTEST BUG FOUND AND FIXED while verifying this. The first version\nasserted only that requiredShipBy was non-nil, and a mutant that dropped\nthe field entirely SURVIVED -- a zero time.Time still serialises to a\nstring, so the nil-check passed while the adapter silently fell back to\nthe earliest-window promise. The assertion now compares against the\ncaller''s actual deadline, and the same mutant fails.\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0%.","tree":{"sha":"34fc30875b40928f10fd7e8a3799126270d0ff07","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/34fc30875b40928f10fd7e8a3799126270d0ff07"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88","verification":{"payload":"tree 34fc30875b40928f10fd7e8a3799126270d0ff07\nparent a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790129897 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790129897 -0300\n\nfeat(planner): send requiredShipBy and let order-management judge feasibility (#3)\n\nCloses the HONEST LIMITATION documented in #2. order-management now\naccepts requiredShipBy on intake (its ADR 0020 phase 4, PR #83), so this\nadapter sends the network''s deadline and takes the verdict rather than\ncomputing its own.\n\nBefore: we sent no deadline, order-management returned the EARLIEST\npromise it could make, and this adapter compared two instants. That\nworked, but it reimplemented the rule here and produced the\nearliest-window answer rather than FeasibleBy''s latest-window one --\ngiving up the floor slack ADR 0020 §2 exists to preserve.\n\nAfter: the deadline goes in the request. A promise back means\norder-management committed to a departure at or before it; no promise\nmeans it could not. An absent promiseDate is now a VERDICT, not a\nmissing field -- for an ordinary order a null promise means \"not\ncomputed yet\", but for an order carrying a deadline it means \"we cannot\nmeet it\".\n\nWe deliberately do NOT re-check the returned date against the deadline.\nA second opinion computed here could only ever disagree with the\nauthority, and disagreeing with the authority is how two services end up\npromising different things. The test table asserts exactly this: a\npromise dated after our deadline still reads feasible.\n\nTEST BUG FOUND AND FIXED while verifying this. The first version\nasserted only that requiredShipBy was non-nil, and a mutant that dropped\nthe field entirely SURVIVED -- a zero time.Time still serialises to a\nstring, so the nil-check passed while the adapter silently fell back to\nthe earliest-window promise. The assertion now compares against the\ncaller''s actual deadline, and the same mutant fails.\n\nmake check green, lint 0 issues, coverage 91.0%.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqszbpCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAApxUQAEox9fuRrdhqUMk1v8iYI+H1\n67LvbZAhSX/zgA80WOutEObHUwRWeE4DV2Zr3+/AtzUp30aNaU/HF235Ikmy9BnN\nJfhiPe+TxgrRl2r91yg96Yv6Kxy0kcmeSd3pN1uJKlV1i5niNOJF4UIJloXNEXpH\n+YTiYgrZSLTy94vxaDiWKGtNIAqwrhsf6PfNd5XHNwKZP21iyfHKLvUvpYaYyyIt\nQcdwqpmi/UObRYrX4+85PkHyHyl6iOwirAZBqIAXNmYR2AuI+J6NUH0PvJfBnWYh\nycXvZusxIsZP0WJ3YdaTKjPD5u+1wrDVLlVxpHxS8jQnIAUgaEGJlvVcEReM7jPa\n6izTnkB8GUcz1pCmpfVHp8VAhzLGS3AE3Y40g8MDLJ4mDknW853foZsvUOUFooyI\nMqevM8aVnAJy9UtT2w8OgyMnW+b8OaLemA4Fn/m3gLx9gXZpoxD+4GovJNMeRUzC\n4BmcCzdKlEufA+HLas9ARTZSvW1eF6QGwGF9bJr9f0tHZ5c2hT0zeALTLvhiwppe\nsq2QRPEL51HdGQqXBaYzC/Y9KiW7uUgkb4vZtDw55M3iBnGY6rIHVVCJ+Uhoo5zy\n45BL+C4iJGdN3/BreKikm4373ghtQBRWoeoDsdR2Q1yxtD+TAJPM4vM9pKu7IqUH\nwQUHMR8cmnbs1KP/B4n1\n=B6xQ\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T02:18:18Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88","node_id":"C_kwDOUmckp9oAKGY1OTM4YzRiYzJmMWE0OGNkN2NmYzFkZjg1YzkyZWQwYjI5MGFkODg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8","sha":"a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8"}],"sha":"f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/f5938c4bc2f1a48cd7cfc1df85c92ed0b290ad88"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T01:52:34Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T01:52:34Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: NetworkOrder skeleton, ACL boundary and active CI (ADR 0001 phase 3) (#2)\n\nThe first service code in this repo. Hexagonal scaffold matching the\nfleet''s shape, the NetworkOrder aggregate, the Anti-Corruption Layer,\nand NETWORK_MODE=stub. No live network call anywhere, per the ADR''s\nrollout step 3.\n\nDOMAIN\n- NetworkOrder aggregate: NEW -> ACKNOWLEDGED|REJECTED -> CONFIRMED,\n  with the invariants ADR 0001 §1 names -- answered exactly once, never\n  partially; no shipment confirmation before acknowledgement; a local\n  order linked only once and only when acknowledged.\n- The acknowledgement deadline is derived from when demand ARRIVED, not\n  from an ambient clock: a poll returning a batch after an outage may\n  carry orders whose 24h windows are already near closing.\n- SKUQuantities() is the narrow doorway inward -- its return type\n  mentions no network identifier at all, which is what makes the Amazon\n  vocabulary structurally unable to leak into order-management.\n- ReceiveUntranslatable is a separate constructor rather than Receive\n  with empty lines: the ErrNoLines invariant protects every other path,\n  and naming the one legitimate lineless order keeps the exception\n  visible instead of weakening the rule for everybody.\n\nAPPLICATION\n- ReceiveNetworkDemand: translate -> raise a HELD order in\n  order-management -> answer the network -> commit. Feasibility is ASKED,\n  never recomputed here (ADR 0001 §7); this use case contains no\n  arithmetic on cutoffs or capacities.\n- Ordering is deliberate on both branches. Acknowledge: save BEFORE\n  telling the network, release LAST, so a crash leaves a recoverable\n  record rather than an acknowledgement the network believes and we have\n  no trace of, and nothing reaches the floor until every fallible step\n  has passed. Reject: cancel the hold BEFORE submitting, because a cancel\n  skipped on an error path is the orphaned-hold failure both ADRs flagged\n  as their honest open gap.\n- Idempotent on repeated polls: the same demand WILL arrive again, and\n  re-answering would duplicate an acknowledgement.\n- SweepAcknowledgementDeadlines closes that orphaned-hold gap: an\n  unanswered order past its window has its hold cancelled and is\n  rejected. It deliberately does NOT acknowledge late -- the network has\n  already re-sourced the order, and confirming would commit us to a\n  shipment nobody expects. One failing order does not abort the pass.\n\nADAPTERS\n- NETWORK_MODE parses fail-CLOSED: anything unrecognised, including\n  empty, is stub. A typo in a deployment manifest must never be the\n  reason this service starts submitting real acknowledgements to a real\n  retailer. sandbox/live return an explicit error rather than silently\n  degrading -- a deployment that ASKED for a real network and got a fake\n  one would look healthy while answering nobody.\n- ordermanagement.Planner speaks its REST API, VERIFIED against the spec\n  on origin/develop rather than assumed. Cancellation is\n  DELETE /orders/{id}, not the POST sub-resource I first wrote; that\n  invented endpoint compiled fine and would have 404''d exactly where\n  inventory gets freed.\n- Honest limitation documented on RaiseHeldOrder: order-management''s\n  FeasibleBy is NOT reachable over REST, because POST /orders accepts no\n  deadline. We compare its returned promise to the deadline instead,\n  which gives the earliest-window answer rather than FeasibleBy''s\n  latest-window one, and gives up that slack. Closing it means adding\n  requiredShipBy to order-management''s intake contract -- its own ADR.\n\nHARNESS\n- ci.yml is ACTIVE, resolving the deferral AGENTS.md recorded. Five jobs\n  whose surfaces actually exist: lint, test, mutation-fast, vuln,\n  arch-test. The template''s other ten were DROPPED, not disabled -- a job\n  failing for want of a features/ or charts/ directory is noise. Each\n  comes back with the PR that creates its surface.\n- .gremlins.yaml thresholds are MEASURED, not copied: efficacy 99,\n  mutant-coverage 92, set below a real run of this repo''s own code\n  (100.00% / 93.33%, 14 killed / 0 lived).\n- Ports stayed interfaces-only by moving the port DTOs to\n  internal/application/contract, rather than dropping the harness rule\n  the way labor-performance had to for its pre-existing code.\n\nmake check green (lint 0 issues, race clean), coverage 91.0% under CI''s\nexact -coverpkg command against a 90% gate, domain 100%, mutation 100%\nefficacy with zero survivors. Boots with no configuration and serves\n/healthz; NETWORK_MODE=live refuses to start.","tree":{"sha":"b2be25498a916efeeb9c74eb2de0e4ed445dc3d6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/b2be25498a916efeeb9c74eb2de0e4ed445dc3d6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8","verification":{"payload":"tree b2be25498a916efeeb9c74eb2de0e4ed445dc3d6\nparent 8a43d0e2a9efa98656bcc11ddb73793246ce2ee3\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790128354 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790128354 -0300\n\nfeat: NetworkOrder skeleton, ACL boundary and active CI (ADR 0001 phase 3) (#2)\n\nThe first service code in this repo. Hexagonal scaffold matching the\nfleet''s shape, the NetworkOrder aggregate, the Anti-Corruption Layer,\nand NETWORK_MODE=stub. No live network call anywhere, per the ADR''s\nrollout step 3.\n\nDOMAIN\n- NetworkOrder aggregate: NEW -> ACKNOWLEDGED|REJECTED -> CONFIRMED,\n  with the invariants ADR 0001 §1 names -- answered exactly once, never\n  partially; no shipment confirmation before acknowledgement; a local\n  order linked only once and only when acknowledged.\n- The acknowledgement deadline is derived from when demand ARRIVED, not\n  from an ambient clock: a poll returning a batch after an outage may\n  carry orders whose 24h windows are already near closing.\n- SKUQuantities() is the narrow doorway inward -- its return type\n  mentions no network identifier at all, which is what makes the Amazon\n  vocabulary structurally unable to leak into order-management.\n- ReceiveUntranslatable is a separate constructor rather than Receive\n  with empty lines: the ErrNoLines invariant protects every other path,\n  and naming the one legitimate lineless order keeps the exception\n  visible instead of weakening the rule for everybody.\n\nAPPLICATION\n- ReceiveNetworkDemand: translate -> raise a HELD order in\n  order-management -> answer the network -> commit. Feasibility is ASKED,\n  never recomputed here (ADR 0001 §7); this use case contains no\n  arithmetic on cutoffs or capacities.\n- Ordering is deliberate on both branches. Acknowledge: save BEFORE\n  telling the network, release LAST, so a crash leaves a recoverable\n  record rather than an acknowledgement the network believes and we have\n  no trace of, and nothing reaches the floor until every fallible step\n  has passed. Reject: cancel the hold BEFORE submitting, because a cancel\n  skipped on an error path is the orphaned-hold failure both ADRs flagged\n  as their honest open gap.\n- Idempotent on repeated polls: the same demand WILL arrive again, and\n  re-answering would duplicate an acknowledgement.\n- SweepAcknowledgementDeadlines closes that orphaned-hold gap: an\n  unanswered order past its window has its hold cancelled and is\n  rejected. It deliberately does NOT acknowledge late -- the network has\n  already re-sourced the order, and confirming would commit us to a\n  shipment nobody expects. One failing order does not abort the pass.\n\nADAPTERS\n- NETWORK_MODE parses fail-CLOSED: anything unrecognised, including\n  empty, is stub. A typo in a deployment manifest must never be the\n  reason this service starts submitting real acknowledgements to a real\n  retailer. sandbox/live return an explicit error rather than silently\n  degrading -- a deployment that ASKED for a real network and got a fake\n  one would look healthy while answering nobody.\n- ordermanagement.Planner speaks its REST API, VERIFIED against the spec\n  on origin/develop rather than assumed. Cancellation is\n  DELETE /orders/{id}, not the POST sub-resource I first wrote; that\n  invented endpoint compiled fine and would have 404''d exactly where\n  inventory gets freed.\n- Honest limitation documented on RaiseHeldOrder: order-management''s\n  FeasibleBy is NOT reachable over REST, because POST /orders accepts no\n  deadline. We compare its returned promise to the deadline instead,\n  which gives the earliest-window answer rather than FeasibleBy''s\n  latest-window one, and gives up that slack. Closing it means adding\n  requiredShipBy to order-management''s intake contract -- its own ADR.\n\nHARNESS\n- ci.yml is ACTIVE, resolving the deferral AGENTS.md recorded. Five jobs\n  whose surfaces actually exist: lint, test, mutation-fast, vuln,\n  arch-test. The template''s other ten were DROPPED, not disabled -- a job\n  failing for want of a features/ or charts/ directory is noise. Each\n  comes back with the PR that creates its surface.\n- .gremlins.yaml thresholds are MEASURED, not copied: efficacy 99,\n  mutant-coverage 92, set below a real run of this repo''s own code\n  (100.00% / 93.33%, 14 killed / 0 lived).\n- Ports stayed interfaces-only by moving the port DTOs to\n  internal/application/contract, rather than dropping the harness rule\n  the way labor-performance had to for its pre-existing code.\n\nmake check green (lint 0 issues, race clean), coverage 91.0% under CI''s\nexact -coverpkg command against a 90% gate, domain 100%, mutation 100%\nefficacy with zero survivors. Boots with no configuration and serves\n/healthz; NETWORK_MODE=live refuses to start.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqszDiCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAeWwQAI7H4/sJwidoeX3hmtXdLOuC\nqiM6qfSj0lfurl/F5BR51cjVN7erWkRZ5d45lVzyn+ZRuNqkwdguEu77l857DHm9\npDts2jHgtwJM1/h7stSalbm1sxm+26XBfmdVfj7r6lBTOaSsEqmLPwaJVVaEY7Tw\nYdER3fjzUHD69ZGLifTWeZAFJexPbN41fL+c2G+ol2KlAsvA0PH2lTleBBVfbHMI\n4iFx/FI7kHVWjPF37F9HV5cN+Bea99P7AKD49DwVF/1gBEGJKouziPnlK4QEmXTM\nBXYJ5rIzrujg8c7n5nXbfBzFXAbe2s3EYjNH1rAMb8zFaCU40/BIzcTVV1/WxuiA\nagw9x0EoRkadwM8KK9UNldgn7ARht3KrltBpZON+ABo9pAIyh2aURxUX8am0+TRR\n+8+OFx7ux3m/zR08yCNY5q0CG1x9VAbpu7ndhELDhI1tSDIvEl7XaJQKxBg4f7p+\nW/HXRiQDfLe1DtuUDQsHXulyOm7r0SRNx/ol2HJOI+uHqyUwMuhJQRs0D4hvXTVY\nBM85QITLdYUh6uVkPoTG1+PX3yZYdCo4WZeKKXRjsQ8PLD+RDGcdXJMdFePJYCyx\n8riWJsmWf2fRtC5rYbxtx443/AZcyQXsiGEVNMlYO9YlK1/h6DGcYlZBcwUSTwFO\nK/9jCsDy/s2CCwTlquDe\n=LwS0\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T01:52:34Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8","node_id":"C_kwDOUmckp9oAKGEyZGYyYzQyMzkwNjljOWZlMjFhYWNlMTQ2NDIwYjNmZDQ1NGQ5ZTg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commit/8a43d0e2a9efa98656bcc11ddb73793246ce2ee3","sha":"8a43d0e2a9efa98656bcc11ddb73793246ce2ee3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/8a43d0e2a9efa98656bcc11ddb73793246ce2ee3"}],"sha":"a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/a2df2c4239069c9fe21aace146420b3fd454d9e8"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4073304?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/claudioed","id":4073304,"login":"claudioed","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQwNzMzMDQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/claudioed","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/commits/8a43d0e2a9efa98656bcc11ddb73793246ce2ee3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-23T00:42:51Z","email":"claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com","name":"Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-23T00:42:51Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs(adr): network-fulfillment as a bounded context (ADR 0001) (#1)\n\n* docs(adr): network-fulfillment as a bounded context (ADR 0001)\n\nCompanion to order-management ADR 0020 (network-originated demand).\nNeither is meaningful without the other; raised together so the\nboundary can be accepted or rejected as one decision.\n\nThe finding this record is built around: there is NO operation in the\nnetwork''s API that accepts a declaration of capability. Cutoffs and\ncycle times are Vendor Central configuration, not an API surface.\nCapability reaches a network only through three consequence signals —\ninventory update, order acknowledgement, shipment confirmation — and\nthe network infers our capability from the gap between the last two.\nSo \"sell capability to the network\" is a decision about what to\nadvertise and what to commit to, not a REST-mapping exercise.\n\nThe one genuinely new domain concept: advertised availability\nconstrained by THROUGHPUT, not just by stock. Most integrations of\nthis shape feed inventory updates straight from physical stock on\nhand; this fleet already publishes cycleTimeP95, the CPT schedule and\nremaining path capacity, so it can advertise\nmin(physicalAvailable, throughputFeasibleBefore(nextCutoff)) instead.\nCapabilityOffer.basis records which rule produced the number, so a\nthrottled advertisement is auditable rather than a silent haircut.\n\nWhy a separate context rather than an adapter in order-management:\nConformist upstream (zero influence over the network''s contract), the\nfleet''s only customer PII (restricted-data ship-to details), the\nfleet''s only external SLA (24h fill-or-kill acknowledgement), and\nadvertised availability having no home on the Order aggregate.\n\nTwo collisions with shipped fleet behaviour are resolved rather than\ndiscovered later: fill-or-kill vs. ADR 0017''s per-shipment-group\npromising, and commit-before-work vs. ADR 0005''s folded\nreceive-allocate-release saga (ADR 0004 makes release the\ncancellation boundary, so a rejected order must never have been\nreleased).\n\nFeasibility is asked of order-management''s PromisePolicy.FeasibleBy\nrather than recomputed here — same reasoning ADR 0014 used when it\ndeclined to move promise computation into wes-work-planning.\n\nVendor Direct Fulfillment chosen over External Fulfillment (deferred\non maturity, not design — NetworkOrder''s vocabulary is deliberately\nprogram-neutral so the swap is an adapter change) and over MCF-as-\nseller (opposite direction entirely).\n\nDocs only. No implementation in this change.\n\n* chore: adopt warehouse-harness-template v1\n\nThe repo was created with a bare README + ADR and skipped the fleet''s\ncanonical harness entirely. This instantiates\n`claudioed/warehouse-harness-template` (develop) properly, via its own\n`scripts/new-service.sh networkfulfillment networkorder`, rather than\nhand-copying pieces.\n\nAdopted verbatim from the template:\n\n- `Makefile` (check / check-all / coverage / arch-test / bdd /\n  integration / mutation-fast / mutation-full / vuln)\n- `internal/architecture/{architecture_test.go,fitness_test.go}` —\n  arch-go''s 6 universal hexagonal-dependency rules plus the 3 fleet\n  invariant tests (no auth reintroduction, no hardcoded Kafka consumer\n  groups, testcontainers required)\n- `.golangci.yml`, `.gremlins.yaml`, `lefthook.yml`, `.gitignore`\n- `.claude/rules/` skeletons and `.claude/skills/how-to-*.md`,\n  `.claude/commands/*.md`\n- `HARNESS.md` — the sensor/guide manifest (what each gate costs, where\n  in the lifecycle it runs, and the real incident behind every fitness\n  rule)\n- `scripts/coverage-quality.py`\n\nInstantiation corrections applied on top:\n\n- `go.mod` module path -> `github.com/claudioed/network-fulfillment`\n  (new-service.sh does not rewrite it, by design).\n- Dropped `scripts/new-service.sh` and `scripts/harness-audit.py` —\n  both belong to the template repo itself, not to an instantiated\n  service. `inventory-storage` keeps only `coverage-quality.py`, which\n  is now the shape here too.\n- Dropped the template''s own `SPEC.md` and `README.md` boilerplate; this\n  repo''s README is real content.\n- Added `AGENTS.md` recording `harness-template: v1` and the eight hard\n  rules for this context (ACL vocabulary boundary, PII containment, no\n  duplicated promise math, persisted correlation mapping, ship-complete\n  network demand, NETWORK_MODE default stub, unique per-process consumer\n  groups, async submission reconciliation).\n\n**`.github/workflows/ci.yml` is deliberately left as `ci.yml.template`.**\nSame reasoning the template repo applies to itself: GitHub only executes\nfiles literally named `*.yml`/`*.yaml` under `.github/workflows/`, and a\nrepo with no Dockerfile, no `apis/openapi.yaml`, no charts and no `web/`\nfails nearly every job. That red would be meaningless noise on every\nfuture commit rather than a useful signal. The placeholder substitution\nHAS been applied to it, so activation is a single `git mv` in the PR that\nlands the first real service code (ADR rollout Phase 3). AGENTS.md says\nso explicitly so it is not forgotten.\n\n`.gremlins.yaml`''s `{{MEASURED_EFFICACY_MINUS_1}}` /\n`{{MEASURED_MUTANT_COVERAGE_MINUS_1}}` are left unsubstituted by design —\nthey must be measured against real code via `make mutation-full`, never\ncopied from a sibling repo. `make mutation` fails loudly until then,\nwhich is the intended behaviour.\n\nVerified locally: `go test ./internal/architecture/...` passes (the\nfitness tests are correct-and-trivial on an empty tree), `go vet ./...`\nclean, `gofmt -l .` empty, `ci.yml.template` parses as valid YAML, and\nthe only remaining placeholders are the two documented gremlins ones.\n\n* chore: record develop branch protection and its deferred status checks\n\nBranch protection is now live on develop, matching\nprocess-path-management''s shape (the most recent bounded context, and a\ncloser analogue than order-management''s leaner two-context rule):\nenforce_admins, required_conversation_resolution, dismiss_stale_reviews,\nno force-push, no deletion, 0 required reviewers.\n\nrequired_status_checks is deliberately null for now. The sibling requires\neight contexts (lint/test/bdd/integration/mutation-fast/vuln/api-lint/\narch-test) with strict:true, but this repo''s ci.yml is still\nci.yml.template, so none of those contexts can ever report — requiring\nthem today would make every PR, including the open ADR PR, permanently\nunmergeable.\n\nAGENTS.md now records this as explicit debt paired with the ci.yml\nactivation, so both halves land in the same Phase 3 PR rather than one\nbeing forgotten.\n\n* docs(adr): accept ADR 0001\n\nAccepted 2026-09-23 alongside order-management ADR 0020, its companion.\nBoth flipped in the same session so implementation never begins against\na record still nominally Proposed on one side of the boundary.","tree":{"sha":"c472532a1d6354641f7a87e12aa4bd140cf22eea","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/trees/c472532a1d6354641f7a87e12aa4bd140cf22eea"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/IQVO/network-fulfillment/git/commits/8a43d0e2a9efa98656bcc11ddb73793246ce2ee3","verification":{"payload":"tree c472532a1d6354641f7a87e12aa4bd140cf22eea\nparent 12f910c8d1d6d5885acddfee36675d3383f52fab\nauthor Claudio Eduardo de Oliveira <claudioed.oliveira@gmail.com> 1790124171 -0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790124171 -0300\n\ndocs(adr): network-fulfillment as a bounded context (ADR 0001) (#1)\n\n* docs(adr): network-fulfillment as a bounded context (ADR 0001)\n\nCompanion to order-management ADR 0020 (network-originated demand).\nNeither is meaningful without the other; raised together so the\nboundary can be accepted or rejected as one decision.\n\nThe finding this record is built around: there is NO operation in the\nnetwork''s API that accepts a declaration of capability. Cutoffs and\ncycle times are Vendor Central configuration, not an API surface.\nCapability reaches a network only through three consequence signals —\ninventory update, order acknowledgement, shipment confirmation — and\nthe network infers our capability from the gap between the last two.\nSo \"sell capability to the network\" is a decision about what to\nadvertise and what to commit to, not a REST-mapping exercise.\n\nThe one genuinely new domain concept: advertised availability\nconstrained by THROUGHPUT, not just by stock. Most integrations of\nthis shape feed inventory updates straight from physical stock on\nhand; this fleet already publishes cycleTimeP95, the CPT schedule and\nremaining path capacity, so it can advertise\nmin(physicalAvailable, throughputFeasibleBefore(nextCutoff)) instead.\nCapabilityOffer.basis records which rule produced the number, so a\nthrottled advertisement is auditable rather than a silent haircut.\n\nWhy a separate context rather than an adapter in order-management:\nConformist upstream (zero influence over the network''s contract), the\nfleet''s only customer PII (restricted-data ship-to details), the\nfleet''s only external SLA (24h fill-or-kill acknowledgement), and\nadvertised availability having no home on the Order aggregate.\n\nTwo collisions with shipped fleet behaviour are resolved rather than\ndiscovered later: fill-or-kill vs. ADR 0017''s per-shipment-group\npromising, and commit-before-work vs. ADR 0005''s folded\nreceive-allocate-release saga (ADR 0004 makes release the\ncancellation boundary, so a rejected order must never have been\nreleased).\n\nFeasibility is asked of order-management''s PromisePolicy.FeasibleBy\nrather than recomputed here — same reasoning ADR 0014 used when it\ndeclined to move promise computation into wes-work-planning.\n\nVendor Direct Fulfillment chosen over External Fulfillment (deferred\non maturity, not design — NetworkOrder''s vocabulary is deliberately\nprogram-neutral so the swap is an adapter change) and over MCF-as-\nseller (opposite direction entirely).\n\nDocs only. No implementation in this change.\n\n* chore: adopt warehouse-harness-template v1\n\nThe repo was created with a bare README + ADR and skipped the fleet''s\ncanonical harness entirely. This instantiates\n`claudioed/warehouse-harness-template` (develop) properly, via its own\n`scripts/new-service.sh networkfulfillment networkorder`, rather than\nhand-copying pieces.\n\nAdopted verbatim from the template:\n\n- `Makefile` (check / check-all / coverage / arch-test / bdd /\n  integration / mutation-fast / mutation-full / vuln)\n- `internal/architecture/{architecture_test.go,fitness_test.go}` —\n  arch-go''s 6 universal hexagonal-dependency rules plus the 3 fleet\n  invariant tests (no auth reintroduction, no hardcoded Kafka consumer\n  groups, testcontainers required)\n- `.golangci.yml`, `.gremlins.yaml`, `lefthook.yml`, `.gitignore`\n- `.claude/rules/` skeletons and `.claude/skills/how-to-*.md`,\n  `.claude/commands/*.md`\n- `HARNESS.md` — the sensor/guide manifest (what each gate costs, where\n  in the lifecycle it runs, and the real incident behind every fitness\n  rule)\n- `scripts/coverage-quality.py`\n\nInstantiation corrections applied on top:\n\n- `go.mod` module path -> `github.com/claudioed/network-fulfillment`\n  (new-service.sh does not rewrite it, by design).\n- Dropped `scripts/new-service.sh` and `scripts/harness-audit.py` —\n  both belong to the template repo itself, not to an instantiated\n  service. `inventory-storage` keeps only `coverage-quality.py`, which\n  is now the shape here too.\n- Dropped the template''s own `SPEC.md` and `README.md` boilerplate; this\n  repo''s README is real content.\n- Added `AGENTS.md` recording `harness-template: v1` and the eight hard\n  rules for this context (ACL vocabulary boundary, PII containment, no\n  duplicated promise math, persisted correlation mapping, ship-complete\n  network demand, NETWORK_MODE default stub, unique per-process consumer\n  groups, async submission reconciliation).\n\n**`.github/workflows/ci.yml` is deliberately left as `ci.yml.template`.**\nSame reasoning the template repo applies to itself: GitHub only executes\nfiles literally named `*.yml`/`*.yaml` under `.github/workflows/`, and a\nrepo with no Dockerfile, no `apis/openapi.yaml`, no charts and no `web/`\nfails nearly every job. That red would be meaningless noise on every\nfuture commit rather than a useful signal. The placeholder substitution\nHAS been applied to it, so activation is a single `git mv` in the PR that\nlands the first real service code (ADR rollout Phase 3). AGENTS.md says\nso explicitly so it is not forgotten.\n\n`.gremlins.yaml`''s `{{MEASURED_EFFICACY_MINUS_1}}` /\n`{{MEASURED_MUTANT_COVERAGE_MINUS_1}}` are left unsubstituted by design —\nthey must be measured against real code via `make mutation-full`, never\ncopied from a sibling repo. `make mutation` fails loudly until then,\nwhich is the intended behaviour.\n\nVerified locally: `go test ./internal/architecture/...` passes (the\nfitness tests are correct-and-trivial on an empty tree), `go vet ./...`\nclean, `gofmt -l .` empty, `ci.yml.template` parses as valid YAML, and\nthe only remaining placeholders are the two documented gremlins ones.\n\n* chore: record develop branch protection and its deferred status checks\n\nBranch protection is now live on develop, matching\nprocess-path-management''s shape (the most recent bounded context, and a\ncloser analogue than order-management''s leaner two-context rule):\nenforce_admins, required_conversation_resolution, dismiss_stale_reviews,\nno force-push, no deletion, 0 required reviewers.\n\nrequired_status_checks is deliberately null for now. The sibling requires\neight contexts (lint/test/bdd/integration/mutation-fast/vuln/api-lint/\narch-test) with strict:true, but this repo''s ci.yml is still\nci.yml.template, so none of those contexts can ever report — requiring\nthem today would make every PR, including the open ADR PR, permanently\nunmergeable.\n\nAGENTS.md now records this as explicit debt paired with the ci.yml\nactivation, so both halves land in the same Phase 3 PR rather than one\nbeing forgotten.\n\n* docs(adr): accept ADR 0001\n\nAccepted 2026-09-23 alongside order-management ADR 0020, its companion.\nBoth flipped in the same session so implementation never begins against\na record still nominally Proposed on one side of the boundary.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsyCLCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/C8QALHh4KgXCdNXQ7JiRp1y2iKp\nGdl7W9I5v+LFaWL2Iu5bF9dHuQ4pyWs9N7RaxDhbD4IjkKszV4H/q3ML7C/crM4T\nzYwzRtkqBeLo7tWf5QETu0P7wn9n0tUnRJwDZp6gZ6kCZfEQBg7BrzUxX315fJia\ny8lWxIoMPzfVuCRc1nuZnUdzc3+/rNGpUltRzPBXsKK9ZDAh/MWvIuf2raayruJG\nLz4RH6RVpYHdg25IXvl3qYDp0/AxSADPexHMLN4mSoqH7RXh7zfYkmG7UInGdhXV\nNhreo37IrkHUOsq2Fwtsn0hhLJHB9vD+kaJBsddxDDvTWKEECcGaUByTphlexcLI\nKX1zU5fVGmaCXpV1BJTmd6qgWtuH5irLuvBsdiR+h44Q2ANLhRhCw7syGc7LB/zB\naDhXW/76iQB3z6KoOnvgJX2lTM5R5iR6dkx65O6k2hCiA4ghYvPaRoiB20s5sJ2o\nYqCcKAuTYfRxQJDbfNIuIYzhFohm2M9rdnflICBBu0spVA4XtxB3ZCSIU/Ygwovt\nHaR9Z3R6k1Ki/k2Gz6g+8oLh/PK55nxT+97j+UiDxLrBfvldhXHCZVaJ+7LVB0k0\n43n4COmYoC4Z5vWfN19xsO4N4rhCl5BSlJUAu5Aas16Qa8MSXgsEDVkYUFgQwWSo\nfPY5cUlmcu7p4fqm69oI\n=t8NO\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-23T00:42:52Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","